  "final": "Both exporters now go through one shared function, and the output strings are unchanged. `python3 checks/public.py` passes, and so does a new `checks/exporter_checks.py`.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **New `src/exporter.py`** contains `export_rows(rows, format)`:\n  - **Normalization:** builds a new dict per row with only `id` and `name`. Missing or `None` values become `''` and anything else is turned into a string. It processes rows one at a time, in order, so it works with one-shot iterables, and the input rows are never changed.\n  - **CSV:** standard `csv.writer` quoting with LF line endings. It always writes the `id,name` header, and every line ends with `\\n`.\n  - **JSON:** `json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False, separators=(\",\", \":\"))`, with no trailing newline, as before.\n  - **Unsupported formats** (including non-string values like `None`) raise `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")`, the same message the dispatcher already used.\n- **`src/csv_exporter.py` and `src/json_exporter.py`** are now one-line wrappers that call `export_rows(rows, \"csv\")` and `export_rows(rows, \"json\")`. Their copied normalization and serialization loops are gone.\n- **`src/dispatcher.py`** is untouched. It still calls the two wrappers, so it now uses the shared function too, and its error behaviour is unchanged.\n\n**What the new check covers:**\n- Exact output from the two wrappers, `export_rows` and the dispatcher, using a non-ASCII name, extra keys, missing and `None` values, and a value with a comma, quote and newline (to test CSV quoting and JSON escaping).\n- Empty input: `\"id,name\\n\"` for CSV and `\"[]\"` for JSON.\n- That the input rows are not modified.\n- One-shot inputs: an iterator and a generator.\n- `ValueError` for `\"xml\"`, `\"CSV\"` and `None`.\n\nI wrote the expected strings by hand from the old code's rules; I didn't run the old version side by side to compare its output directly.",
